  roughly [38;5;153m{ id, title, done, dueDate }[39m.

  One architectural fork left, and it's the one that decides whether "notifications" is a [3mthing we store[0m or [3ma way of [0m
  [3mlooking at tasks[0m.

  [1mThe fork:[0m overdue and due-soon are computable from tasks + the current clock. So we can either derive them on every
  render, or materialize them as notification records.

  - [1mDerived view[0m — no notification storage at all. A function takes your tasks and [38;5;153mnow[39m, returns what needs attention.
    Self-healing: push a due date out and the notification vanishes; complete the task and it's gone. Almost no code,
    nothing to keep in sync, nothing to migrate. The cost: no dismissal — if something is overdue and you're
    deliberately ignoring it, it nags every render.
  - [1mStored records[0m — real notification objects with read/dismissed state. Gets you dismissal and a history. The cost is
    all the machinery around it: deduping so one overdue task doesn't spawn a record per page load, invalidating records
    when the task changes underneath them, and reconciling on load. This is where most of the bugs in this feature
    would live.
  - [1mDerived + snooze[0m — derived view plus one [38;5;153msnoozedUntil[39m field on the task. Dismissal without a notification entity or
    any sync problem.

  Storage is the expensive-to-reverse choice; the other two are a few hours' work and interchangeable. My recommendation
  is derived + snooze: you get the one thing pure derivation lacks for the cost of a single timestamp field.
